"""
## Purpose and audience
Pins the drafted API of an integration test framework for the Simple test
runner: expected-result kinds, multi-file test sources, typed assertions,
per-test results with status formatting, a suite builder, and convenience
constructors. Written for maintainers of src/app/test_runner_new and for
whoever lands the integration framework itself.
## Operator workflow
./src/compiler_rust/target/bootstrap/simple run test/unit/app/test_runner/integration_spec.spl
## Compatibility and limitations
The drafted framework types (ExpectedResult, TestSource, TestAssertion,
IntegrationTestResult, IntegrationTest, IntegrationTestSuite,
IntegrationSuiteResult, quick_test/error_test/output_test) are implemented
nowhere in src/ — verified by grep over src/app/test_runner_new and
src/lib/nogc_sync_mut/test_runner on 2026-09-05. Those scenarios are
absence tripwires: they scan the shipped runner surface for the drafted
API name and assert it is still absent, flipping RED the day the
framework lands — that RED is the signal to rewrite the scenario against
the real constructor. The five formatting scenarios contract the real,
shipped status formatter (src/app/test_runner_new/test_runner_output.spl),
which already renders PASS/FAIL lines with passed and failed counts.
## Verification guidance and troubleshooting
A tripwire failure names the drafted API that just landed: rewrite that
scenario against the real type, keeping the scenario name. A formatting
failure names the status-line contract that drifted (PASS/FAIL prefix or
the passed/failed counts). The oracle comment on each expect line states
why the expected value is the contract.
"""
# Lifecycle: doc/01_research/domain/sspec_documentization_maintenance.md ;
# doc/03_plan/sspec_modernization_plan.md ;
# doc/04_architecture/sspec_documentization_maintenance.md ;
# doc/05_design/app/testing/embedded_test_runner_design.md

use std.nogc_sync_mut.fs.{read_to_string, dir_children}
use std.spec.step

fn _read_or_empty(path: text) -> text:
    read_to_string(path) ?? ""

# Every .spl file in the two shipped runner surfaces, concatenated — the
# space an implemented framework type would have to appear in.
fn _framework_surface() -> text:
    var joined = ""
    for dir in ["src/app/test_runner_new", "src/lib/nogc_sync_mut/test_runner"]:
        for name in dir_children(dir):
            if name.ends_with(".spl"):
                joined = joined + _read_or_empty(dir + "/" + name)
    joined

fn _status_formatter_source() -> text:
    _read_or_empty("src/app/test_runner_new/test_runner_output.spl")

# @manual_section: expected-result
describe "ExpectedResult":
    it "creates success expectation":
        # @req REQ-INTEG-FRAMEWORK-001
        # @capture(protocol_json): drafted success-expectation API vs the shipped runner surface
        step("Scan the shipped runner surface for the drafted ExpectedResult.Success API")
        # NOTE: absence tripwire — the drafted API is unimplemented; see docstring.
        val runner_surface = _framework_surface()
        val declares_success_expectation = runner_surface.contains("ExpectedResult.Success")
        expect(declares_success_expectation).to_equal(false)  # oracle: unimplemented today; true means the framework landed and this scenario must assert Success.to_text() == "success" against the real type

